---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/postscript-alternative';
const title = "Postscript alternatives for Shopify: preserve the texting operation";
const desc = "Compare SMS, RCS, email access and service ownership before moving a Postscript program.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Is Postscript still SMS only?", "answer": "No. It markets SMS, RCS and Email. Confirm email availability and rollout scope for your account, plus the exact supported migration and sending functions, before treating consolidation as available."}, {"question": "Can a merchant keep its number?", "answer": "Confirm ownership, transfer eligibility and the release and activation sequence with both providers. A port does not migrate keywords, permissions, reply routing or pending messages."}, {"question": "What should a replacement demonstration cover?", "answer": "Use a live capture fixture, a waiting cart message interrupted by purchase, and a customer reply through the helpdesk. Require named owners and expected results before any production switch."}, {"question": "Can spend be reduced without migrating?", "answer": "Audit destination rates, encoding, media, campaign eligibility, overlapping recovery and service fees first. Compare an agreed normal and peak workload, including staff work, against the incumbent repair option."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container max-w-4xl">
    <nav class="mb-8"><a class="link" href="/alternatives">Alternatives</a></nav>
    <h1 class="hero-title">Postscript alternatives for Shopify: preserve the texting operation</h1>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14">

      <p>A Postscript replacement must take over a texting operation: capture, sending identity, conversations, automations and the work people do around them. A lower advertised message rate does not establish that handover. The exercises below are proposed procurement checks, not completed tests or merchant results.</p>
      <h2>Reasons to keep and repair Postscript</h2>
      <p>Postscript's <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/features">feature documentation</a> describes Shopify-focused messaging, response handling and helpdesk connections. Its <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/rcs">RCS offering</a> adds richer branded messaging. Keep the incumbent when capture, segmentation, service replies and operating support work well at an acceptable total cost. Inspect the invoice and campaign design before assuming an expensive sale week requires a new supplier.</p>
      <p>The blanket claim that Postscript has no email is stale: <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">Postscript Email</a> is now marketed. Treat access as a rollout question, not an entitlement inferred from a launch page. Ask which phase your store can use, when sending is available, which templates and flows can migrate, and what limits remain. Keeping current email while evaluating this scope can be preferable to moving both channels together.</p>
      <p>For SMS overspend, inspect recipient selection, encoding, message parts, media and destination charges. Check whether a purchase failed to exit cart recovery or the same event activated two tools. Repairing a filter or queue owner may address the problem. Seek replacement when a demonstrated missing destination, integration, service arrangement or budget requirement remains after that repair.</p>
      <h2>Proposed number and sending-identity acceptance</h2>
      <p>Record the current toll-free number or short code, registered brand, sender names, channel capabilities and ownership documents. Postscript's <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564366-what-is-my-phone-number-subscribers-can-text-to-opt-into-my-sms">number guide</a> explains the assigned toll-free number. Ask both providers about transfer eligibility, required approvals, release order, downtime and rollback. Do not promise that a port will complete on a chosen day.</p>
      <p>For RCS, ask which destinations and devices are supported, how brand verification transfers or must be repeated, and how fallback is billed. Prepare a controlled rich-message and fallback exercise with expected sender display, links and reply behavior. A successful SMS port is not acceptance of RCS identity. For any new email stream, inventory the sending domain, authentication, reply address, templates and bounce handling separately.</p>
      <h2>Capture and keyword handover</h2>
      <p>Postscript supports <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564129-create-a-keyword">keyword creation on dedicated numbers</a>. Make a register of printed packaging, QR codes, popup text, landing pages, checkout settings and paid creative that expose each number or keyword. Assign an owner to update each surface; replacing the website popup alone leaves printed invitations pointing at the old service.</p>
      <p>Propose a fresh test signup from each surface. Record the keyword, disclosure version, sender, purpose, channel, timestamp, subscriber record and expected confirmation. Verify current withdrawals and suppressions in the destination before enabling promotions. Preserve permission evidence and keyword history where available, and resolve missing export fields with the provider. Neither a phone field nor email permission authorizes promotional SMS. Confirm applicable destination requirements with the responsible owner rather than treating a copied configuration as universal compliance.</p>
      <h2>Replies and managed work are part of the product</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564169-set-up-your-gladly-integration">Gladly integration guide</a> documents forwarding replies and responding into the SMS thread. Inventory your actual Responses inbox, forwarding address, helpdesk connection, routing rules and unresolved conversations. Send a controlled product question and check which agent receives it, how they answer and what context remains visible. Check withdrawal and help requests separately from ordinary service questions, including after-hours ownership.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/postscript-plus">Postscript Plus</a> offers managed marketing services. If your contract includes strategy or execution, enumerate the tasks being replaced: campaign calendar, copy, segment selection, offer setup, approvals, reporting and escalation. Put a named internal employee, agency or replacement service beside each task and price its hours. A self-service app quote cannot be compared fairly with a managed program until this work has an owner.</p>
      <h2>Accept the waiting messages before switching</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>List campaigns and automations with trigger, event ID, template, offer expiry, queued recipient count and queue owner. Decide which waiting people should finish, be cancelled or be deliberately re-enrolled.</li>
        <li>Propose a cart that purchases before the delayed text, a duplicate event and a late event after the cutover. Require one production sender per purpose and channel and no replay of historical recovery merely because records were imported.</li>
        <li>Exercise a withdrawn subscriber, an unavailable variant, a gift purchase and an open support case. Confirm current permission, inventory, offer and purchase checks at the time the message would send, including relevant timing controls.</li>
        <li>Preview the actual SMS and MMS creative, links, discount expiry and product fallback. Accept the template and its message-part cost together; do not assume a richer creative has the same unit cost.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Set a release checkpoint only after number routing, capture, reply handling and queue disposition pass the agreed fixtures. Pause old production paths and reconcile suppressions before activating replacements. Keep a rollback owner and prevent rollback from replaying already sent steps. Preserve reports and required records, verify cancellation terms and service handover, then retire the old subscription.</p>
      <h2>Procurement and economics</h2>
      <p>For a consolidated email-and-texting proposal, require the bidder to demonstrate both channels and separate permissions using your fixtures. For a specialist proposal, keep the working email platform and identify the cross-channel recovery owner. The <a class="link" href="/alternatives/mailchimp-alternative">Mailchimp alternative brief</a> concerns audience and transactional dependencies; this move instead depends on identity, capture and service continuity. Neither brief establishes a universal winner.</p>
      <p>Request normal-month and BFCM quotes with the same delivered workload, attempted sends, countries, encoding and segments, MMS/RCS mix and fallback assumptions. Include platform minimums, carrier costs, number charges, credits and expiry, email costs, managed services, migration work and internal staffing. Track opt-outs and complaints by flow and capture source, service backlog and incremental contribution. Attributed recovery revenue alone cannot justify the bill.</p>
      <p>Illustrative fixture: a $100 order minus $45 fixed goods cost and $10 other variable costs leaves $45. A $25 offer leaves $20 before messaging; it does not automatically lose money. Thirty incremental discounted orders less $450 of additional program cost leave $150 (30 × $20 − $450), before migration labor. Use your own margin and refund assumptions and a suitable holdout where feasible. Set contact frequency from the promise made at capture and observed response, rather than asserting that a fourth text always fails or a fixed 24-hour gap is safe.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
